# Generated design references to frontend code

This skill retains its image-generation-first website workflow. It is not the default for a supplied screenshot that the user wants reproduced unchanged; use `impeccable` for that fixed-contract reconstruction rather than replacing the reference or stacking design philosophies.

## Essential workflow and constraints

1. **Generate design images → deeply analyze them → implement the extracted design.** For a visual design request in this workflow, generation precedes freeform code when the capability is available. If it is unavailable, report the limitation and resolve the intended reference source; do not pretend images were generated.
2. Generate enough clear, large standalone section images. In Codex, prefer one image per requested section; never compress many sections until typography/components become unreadable. Fresh section/detail rendering is preferred to cropping/zooming an old board.
3. Extract composition, hierarchy, typography, palette, spacing, radii, assets, buttons, alignment, responsive intent and section rhythm. Resolve unclear details with a fresh view/render, not invented approximation.
4. Treat the approved generated references as the visual implementation contract. Preserve copy, proportions, layout and imagery instead of coding a generic reinterpretation. Identify accepted deviations explicitly.
5. Keep first view clean and readable on a small laptop: short headline, visible CTA, coherent navigation and a legible visual. Avoid nested containers/cards, tiny pills, micro-label clutter and fake dashboard decoration.
6. Use the existing project stack and installed dependencies, semantic accessible components, responsive behavior and appropriate current verification. User requirements/approved direction outrank generic aesthetic defaults.

